cards.body:
The employer and the applicable partner jointly write a **work place harassment and violence prevention policy**. It must include, among other things:

- the employer's **mission statement** on prevention and protection;
- the **roles** of the employer, designated recipient, employees and committees or representative;
- the **risk factors** identified;
- a **summary of the training**;
- a **summary of the resolution process**, naming the **designated recipient** and explaining how to give notice of an occurrence;
- a summary of **emergency procedures**;
- how the **privacy** of people involved will be protected;
- other **recourse** available and the **support measures** offered;
- the person designated to receive **complaints** under section 127.1 of the Code.

The policy must be **available to all employees**, reviewed jointly **at least every three years** and updated after any change to one of its elements.

OSFI expects behavioural expectations to be written down in documents such as **codes of conduct** and **conflict of interest** policies, and communicated clearly to employees, contractors and stakeholders, including how breaches will be handled.

A code of conduct should:

- **apply to all employees** and **include regular training**;
- stress **following the law**, regulatory expectations and internal policies;
- stress **avoiding conflicts of interest**, such as bribery and other improper influence;
- stress **objectivity** and avoiding bias in decisions;
- stress **security and confidentiality** of assets, communications and information;
- cover how real, potential and perceived conflicts are **detected, disclosed, avoided and managed**.

OSFI also expects codes to be **assessed for effectiveness** and updated regularly, and conformity to be **monitored based on risk**, considering each role's exposure to undue influence. The Corporate Governance Guideline adds that the **board approves** codes of ethics and conduct.

**Annual report (section 36).** On or before **1 March** each year, the employer sends the Head of Compliance and Enforcement a report covering occurrences notified in the **previous calendar year**. Besides the employer's name, business number and a contact person, it gives:

- the total number of occurrences;
- how many involved **sexual** and **non-sexual** harassment and violence;
- how many resulted in an employee's death;
- if known, the number linked to each prohibited ground in the Canadian Human Rights Act;
- where they happened and the professional relationship between the parties;
- how each resolution process was completed and the **average time** in months.

**Fatality report (section 37).** If an occurrence results in an employee's death, the employer reports it within **24 hours** of becoming aware of the death, with a general description, the date and time, and a contact person.

Notice that the annual report contains **numbers, not names**: privacy is protected even in reporting.
